WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:09.890
Hi, my name is Mackenzie Manna, an IBM Redbooks project leader specializing in IBM Z.

2
00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:15.890
When you enter a shell session, you are pointed to a particular directory in the file system hierarchy.

3
00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:20.890
This directory is called the Current Working Directory, or CWD.

4
00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:25.890
The value of the Current Working Directory is initially assigned as your home directory

5
00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:34.890
as found in the OMPS segment of your user security profile in the ZOS security system, like RACAF.

6
00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.890
Your home directory is a directory that belongs to you.

7
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.890
It is where you place your personal files and subdirectories.

8
00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:47.890
Your CWD is a directory which you happen to be pointed to at any given time.

9
00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:53.890
The environment variable $Home holds the value of your home directory.

10
00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:58.890
Your home directory never changes and neither does the value of $Home.

11
00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:03.890
But your Current Working Directory might change from time to time.

12
00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:08.890
Many shell commands allow you to specify a path name or a directory name.

13
00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:15.890
If you do not specify a path name or directory name, the command often defaults to your Current Working Directory.

14
00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:19.890
When you enter the eyeshell, you do not have a Current Working Directory.

15
00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:26.890
You can assign yourself a CWD in the eyeshell, and once assigned, you can change it whenever you want.

16
00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:32.890
Path names that are entered in the Unix environment are either absolute or relative path names.

17
00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:39.890
Absolute path names always begin with a slash, and that leading slash indicates the root directory.

18
00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:45.890
Therefore, an absolute path name always begins at search at the root directory.

19
00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.890
Relative path names never begin with a slash.

20
00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:54.890
A relative path name always begins at search at the Current Working Directory.

21
00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:59.890
The CWD is the directory that you are pointed to at any given time.

22
00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:08.890
In the shell, you can point to another directory, that is, change your CWD by using the shell cd command, change directory.

23
00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:17.890
To assign or change a CWD in the eyeshell, type the directory that you want to assign in the path name field on the main menu.

24
00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:24.890
Then, go to the directory action bar item and press enter to pull down the directory menu.

25
00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:29.890
Finally, select the option Set Working Directory.

26
00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:34.890
Alright, let's get into a whole bunch more commonly used shell commands.

27
00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:41.890
The shell cd command allows you to, you know this from a few minutes back, change your working directory.

28
00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:52.890
The cd command produces no output, but the CWD is changed and the environment variable $pwd is updated to reflect that change.

29
00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:58.890
You can specify either a relative path name or an absolute path name on the cd command.

30
00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:04.890
When any directory is created, it contains two entries created by Unix.

31
00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:08.890
These entries are commonly called dot and dot dot.

32
00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:14.890
The dot entry, typed as a single period, represents the directory itself.

33
00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:19.890
This is useful when you want to execute a file in the current working directory.

34
00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:24.890
And that directory is not specified in your $path variable.

35
00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:32.890
Simply type dot slash file name, where file name is the name of the file to be executed.

36
00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:39.890
There are also many commands which require a path name to be entered, for example the cp command.

37
00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:49.890
If you wish to use the current working directory as a path name, simply type the dot rather than typing the absolute path name of the CWD.

38
00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:55.890
The shell mkdir command, make directory, creates a directory for you.

39
00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:05.890
As with the cd command, you can type either an absolute or relative path name to specify the directory that you want to create.

40
00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:14.890
You can also create multiple directories at the same time by specifying multiple path names with a single mkdir command.

41
00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:19.890
A directory cannot be created if there is no path to the directory.

42
00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:35.890
In other words, if you were to type make directory slash u slash TSA-0011 slash DRA slash DIRB and DIRA did not exist, then DIRB cannot be created.

43
00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:44.890
It was for this reason that the ability to create a directory tree structure was developed by using the dash p option of make directory.

44
00:04:44.890 --> 00:05:01.890
Typing make directory dash p slash u slash TSA-0011 slash DIRA slash DIRB creates the necessary directories in the tree so that DIRB is successfully created.

45
00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:12.890
If DIRA did not exist, it is created under TSA-0011 and then DIRB is created under DIRA.

46
00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:18.890
The shell chmod command allows you to change the permission bits or mode of files or directories.

47
00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:25.890
As before, you can enter an absolute or relative path name to identify the files to be changed.

48
00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:31.890
Only a super user or the owner of a file can issue chmod against that file.

49
00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:39.890
You can change multiple files to the same mode by typing multiple path name arguments in the chmod command.

50
00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:51.890
Using the dash r, note this is an uppercase R option, which stands for recursive, allows you to specify a directory and change not only the mode of that directory,

51
00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:56.890
but also all files and subdirectors below that directory.

52
00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:00.890
There is another form of chmod called the symbolic form.

53
00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:06.890
With the symbolic form, you specify exactly which permission bits you want to change.

54
00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:09.890
The rest of the permission bits are left unchanged.

55
00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.890
Who can use the chmod command?

56
00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:24.890
Users with read access to UNIX PRIV class profiles superuser.filesys.changeperms can use the chmod command to change the permission bits of any file.

57
00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:35.890
A super user with uid equals 0 or that has read access to the rackf facility class bpx.superuser and the owner.

58
00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:40.890
The shell sort command sorts input data in many different ways.

59
00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:46.890
The default sort order is an ASCII ascending sort against the first field of the file.

60
00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:51.890
A field is a string of characters delimited by white space, blanks or tabs.

61
00:06:51.890 --> 00:07:02.890
With other options, you can sort a numeric order, dictionary order ignoring case, month by month order, reverse or descending order, and so forth.

62
00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:06.890
You can also select which fields to sort within the file.

63
00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.890
That is what the dash k option does.

64
00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:17.890
The start pos starting position keyword identifies the field by number against which to start the sort.

65
00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:30.890
Entering sort dash k 3 my file sorts the file called my file in ASCII ascending order beginning at the third field of each record in the file.

66
00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:36.890
The shell cat command stands for concatenate, which means to join together.

67
00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:40.890
The cat command joins an input file with an output file.

68
00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:49.890
The first argument passed to the cat is assumed to be an input file path name, and the default output file is the terminal screen.

69
00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:54.890
Therefore, cat can be used to display file content on the screen.

70
00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:59.890
The file content to be displayed might be more than fits on one screen.

71
00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:03.890
In that case, the shell more command might be more appropriate.

72
00:08:03.890 --> 00:08:07.890
The cat command can also be used to copy a file.

73
00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:17.890
The all caps oedit and all caps obrowse commands are both TSO commands, but they also have Unix shell counterparts.

74
00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:22.890
Note that the shell oedit and obrowse commands are both lowercase.

75
00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:29.890
The oedit uppercase and oedit lowercase commands invoke the ISPF editor against a Unix file.

76
00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:33.890
If the file does not exist, it is created dynamically.

77
00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:36.890
You are placed into a true ISPF edit session.

78
00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:43.890
When you save your changes or additions, the data is written into the Unix file you specified on the command.

79
00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:51.890
When you invoke the TSO oedit command, you should specify an absolute path name enclosed with single quotes.

80
00:08:51.890 --> 00:09:01.890
The obrowse uppercase and obrowse lowercase commands invoke ISPF browse, not ISPF view, against a Unix file.

81
00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:04.890
Again, this is a true ISPF browse session.

82
00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:15.890
If you invoke any of these four commands without specifying a path name, you are presented with an ISPF panel where you can enter the path name of the target file.

83
00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:24.890
The oput command copies one sequential data set or one member of a partition data set into an hfs file.

84
00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:31.890
The target file that you specify might not be a directory, and the file is created if it does not already exist.

85
00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:39.890
The oput x command copies all members of a partition data set to files in a specified directory.

86
00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:48.890
The oget command copies one hfs file to a sequential data set or to a member of a partition data set.

87
00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:55.890
If you are copying to a partition data set member, you must specify the member name as well as the data set name.

88
00:09:55.890 --> 00:10:03.890
The oget x command copies all of the files from a specified directory to members of a partition data set.

89
00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:08.890
The member names are the uppercase equivalents of the hfs file names.

90
00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:18.890
If a file name to be copied is longer than 8 characters or uses characters that are not allowed in a zos data set name, that file is not copied.

91
00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:30.890
There are various oprands you can use on these commands to specify that the data to be copied is binary data, text data, and that data conversion should take place.

92
00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:40.890
For example, from the ibm-1047 to the ibm-037 or ASCII code pages.

93
00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:45.890
And there we go, a whole bunch of shell commands that you'll likely be able to make good use of.

94
00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:48.890
In the next module, we'll be working with the shell.
